ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240903 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=5{t}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t+1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240903 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d406e5c-2737-5c6c-bad0-bb58ddb067d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240903 #edb/ref 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)=\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(5{t}^{2}){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}+(5{t}^{2}){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}+(5{t}^{2}){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+0+10 \\\\  & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}+(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}+(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+1+4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}\\cdot (t+1) \\\\  & =2(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240904 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240904 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240904 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240904 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=12({t}^{2}-t)$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240904 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99c91096-e517-53ed-91eb-c1bbb59a1a8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240904 #edb/ref 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2}+12t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[3{t}^{4}-8{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(3-8+6)-0 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =3(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240950 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240950 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104871" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240950 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240950 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $1,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-1,\\,q(t)=4t-3\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t)}$ equals $t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e18df242-9d5f-58ab-883f-e2f596589751" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240950 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =q(0){p}_{2}(0)+q(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(4t-3){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(4t-3){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{2}(0){p}_{2}(0)+{p}_{2}(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}{|}_{0}+(t-1)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{3}{1}\\cdot (t-1)=3t-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =t+(3t-3) \\\\  & =4t-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240955 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240955 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240955 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $2,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-1,\\,q(t)={t}^{2}+t\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{2}(t)}$ equals $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240955 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0a44c8c-788c-548d-8b09-deb0ad2858dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240955 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=0,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩ & =q(-1){p}_{1}(-1)+q(0){p}_{1}(0)+q(1){p}_{1}(1) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(t){|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{1}(-1){p}_{1}(-1)+{p}_{1}(0){p}_{1}(0)+{p}_{1}(1){p}_{1}(1) \\\\  & =(t)^{2}{|}_{-1}+(t)^{2}{|}_{0}+(t)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{2}{2}\\cdot t=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =t+0 \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240962 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240962 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240962 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240962 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[-1,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)={t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}+2$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=5{t}^{2}+1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240962 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a0afbf2-8aa8-5003-9b37-01bec551a919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240962 #edb/ref 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}+2)\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(5{t}^{5}+50{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}+20{t}^{2}+2)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5}{6}{t}^{6}+10{t}^{5}+\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{20}{3}{t}^{3}+2t]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{6}+10+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{20}{3}+2)-(\\frac{5}{6}-10+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{20}{3}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{112}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(5{t}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(25{t}^{4}+10{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[5{t}^{5}+\\frac{10}{3}{t}^{3}+t]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(5+\\frac{10}{3}+1)-(-5-\\frac{10}{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{112}{3})}{(\\frac{56}{3})}\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2(5{t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =10{t}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240978 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240978 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240978 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240978 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$ \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=3t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}+1,\\,g(t)=10{t}^{3}\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $6t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240978 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2794de9d-8dc5-594e-8a2b-e907f92bf64b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240978 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=6t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $10{t}^{3}$ is an odd function, while ${t}^{2}+1$ is even. Hence, their product $10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =6t+0 \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240983 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240983 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105136" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240983 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240983 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240983 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "645a82fe-e42f-5dc9-9e79-d8bd0747a894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240983 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241003 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241003 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105186" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241003 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241003 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_